# 20.8 Chat Session Lifecycle

```text
User Starts New Chat

↓

Create Chat Session

↓

Session Appears in Sidebar

↓

User Sends Messages

↓

AI Responds

↓

Conversation Memory Updated

↓

(Optional)

AI Generates Session Summary

↓

Conversation Continues

↓

Archive / Delete
```

A chat session acts as the long-term container for an entire conversation.

Individual messages remain immutable and are stored separately in the `chat_messages` table.

---

# 20.9 Conversation State

Each session maintains its own conversational context.

The session tracks:

- Conversation title
- AI-generated summary
- Recent interaction timestamp
- Retrieval preferences
- Agent configuration
- Conversation metadata

The actual conversation memory used by the LLM is reconstructed dynamically from the associated chat messages rather than stored as one continuously growing prompt.

This approach keeps context management scalable while supporting long-running conversations.

---

# 20.10 Session Storage Strategy

KnowledgeOS separates conversational data into two layers.

```text
Chat Session

↓

Conversation Metadata

↓

Chat Messages
```

The session stores only lightweight metadata.

Individual user prompts and AI responses are stored inside the `chat_messages` table.

This separation enables:

- Faster chat history loading
- Smaller database rows
- Efficient pagination
- Independent message retrieval
- Easier analytics

---

# 20.11 AI Session Configuration

Every conversation maintains its own AI configuration.

This allows future conversations to behave differently without affecting other chats.

Configuration currently includes:

- Retrieval Enabled
- Planner Agent Enabled
- Reviewer Agent Enabled
- Streaming Enabled

Future versions may support:

- Different LLM providers
- Temperature selection
- Maximum context size
- Custom system prompts
- Tool permissions

---

# 20.12 Conversation Summary

Long conversations may contain hundreds of messages.

Instead of displaying only the first message,

KnowledgeOS periodically generates an AI summary.

Example:

```text
System Design Interview Preparation

Topics Discussed

• Load Balancers

• Caching

• Database Replication

• Message Queues
```

Conversation summaries improve:

- Sidebar previews
- Search
- Recommendations
- Memory compression

Summaries never replace the original conversation history.

---

# 20.13 Conversation Search

Conversation search operates on:

- Title
- AI Summary
- Message Content (future)
- Metadata

Future semantic search may also index conversation summaries inside ChromaDB.

This enables users to quickly locate previous discussions.

