# 20.14 Design Decisions

## Why Separate Sessions from Messages?

Keeping conversation metadata separate from individual messages:

- Reduces row size
- Simplifies queries
- Improves scalability
- Enables efficient pagination

This mirrors the architecture used by many production conversational AI systems.

---

## Why Store Cached Message Count?

Displaying conversation history should not require repeatedly executing expensive COUNT() queries.

The cached `total_messages` field provides instant statistics while remaining synchronized through database triggers.

---

## Why Store Conversation Summary?

Conversation summaries allow:

- Better sidebar previews
- Faster search
- Future memory compression
- Personalized recommendations

without repeatedly processing the full conversation.

---

## Why Store AI Configuration Per Session?

Conversation behavior may evolve over time.

Storing configuration at the session level allows:

- Different retrieval modes
- Different AI models
- Custom prompts
- Experimental features

without changing the overall user profile.

---

## Why Support Soft Delete?

Soft deletion provides:

- Accidental deletion recovery
- Future restore functionality
- Safer data management
- Improved user experience

Deleted conversations remain invisible to the user interface.

---

# 20.15 Business Rules

- Every chat session belongs to exactly one authenticated user.
- Every message belongs to exactly one chat session.
- A user may own unlimited chat sessions.
- Session titles may be renamed by the user.
- Newly created conversations use the default title **"New Chat"** until renamed or automatically summarized.
- Deleting a session permanently deletes all associated messages.
- Archived conversations remain searchable unless explicitly deleted.
- Pinned conversations always appear before non-pinned conversations.
- Soft-deleted conversations are excluded from the application interface.
- AI summaries never replace the original conversation history.
- Retrieval settings are applied independently for each conversation.

---

# 20.16 Future Expansion

Future versions may introduce:

- Conversation branching
- Shared conversations
- Team workspaces
- Conversation export
- Markdown export
- PDF export
- Conversation tags
- Folder organization for chats
- Semantic conversation search
- AI memory compression
- Multi-agent conversations
- Voice conversation history
- Conversation replay

The current schema supports these features without requiring structural redesign.

---

# 20.17 Summary

The `chat_sessions` table serves as the organizational layer for conversational interactions within KnowledgeOS.

By separating lightweight conversation metadata from individual chat messages, the architecture supports scalable conversation management, efficient history loading, AI-generated summaries, configurable retrieval behavior, and future conversational enhancements.

This design ensures that each conversation remains isolated, searchable, extensible, and tightly integrated with the broader Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) pipeline while maintaining excellent performance for long-running AI interactions.

